Here is the C function that creates a Huffman tree from a set of symbol lengths:
```c
int huffman_tree_create(int *symbol_lengths, int length_count) {
    // Create a frequency table for each code length
    int *frequency_table = lodepng_malloc(length_count * sizeof(int));
    if (!frequency_table) return 1; // Allocation failed

    int i;
    for (i = 0; i < length_count; i++) {
        frequency_table[i] = symbol_lengths[i];
    }

    // Find the total number of occurrences
    int total_occurrences = 0;
    for (i = 0; i < length_count; i++) {
        total_occurrences += frequency_table[i];
    }

    // Create the Huffman tree
    HuffmanNode *root = huffman_tree_create_node(NULL, NULL);
    if (!root) return 1; // Allocation failed

    // Insert nodes into the tree
    for (i = 0; i < length_count; i++) {
        root->left = huffman_tree_create_node(root, frequency_table[i]);
        if (!root->left) return 1; // Allocation failed
    }

    return 0; // Success
}

HuffmanNode *huffman_tree_create_node(HuffmanNode *parent, int frequency) {
    HuffmanNode *node = lodepng_malloc(sizeof(HuffmanNode));
    if (!node) return NULL; // Allocation failed

    node->frequency = frequency;
    node->parent = parent;

    return node;
}
```
Note that this implementation assumes a `lodepng_malloc` function is available, which allocates memory for the tree nodes. You may need to modify the code to use a different allocation mechanism if `lodepng_malloc` is not available.

Also, this implementation creates a simple Huffman tree where each node represents a symbol and its frequency of occurrence. The root node of the tree represents the overall distribution of the symbols. The function returns an error code (1) if any memory allocation fails, indicating that the creation of the Huffman tree failed.

