using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

public int ExecuteCommand(string command)
{
    ProcessStartInfo startInfo = new ProcessStartInfo();
    startInfo.FileName = "cmd.exe"; // Use cmd.exe to execute the command
    startInfo.Arguments = "/C " + command; // Add the command to execute
    startInfo.UseShellExecute = false;
    startInfo.RedirectStandardOutput = true;
    startInfo.RedirectStandardError = true;

    Process process = new Process();
    process.StartInfo = startInfo;

    process.OutputDataReceived += (sender, data) => 
    {
        // This is where you would call your callback function
        Console.WriteLine(data.Data);
    };

    process.ErrorDataReceived += (sender, data) => 
    {
        // This is where you would call your callback function for error messages
        Console.WriteLine("Error: " + data.Data);
    };

    process.Start();
    process.BeginOutputReadLine();
    process.BeginErrorReadLine();

    int exitCode = process.WaitForExit(30000); // Wait for 30 seconds

    return exitCode;
}
```

This function will execute the specified command, and then wait for it to finish. The `WaitForExit` method is used to wait for the process to finish, with a timeout of 30 seconds.

Please note that this code assumes you are running on Windows and using cmd.exe to execute the command. If you're running on another platform or want to use a different executable to run your command, you would need to adjust the `FileName` property accordingly.

