using System;
using System.Diagnostics;

public static string ReadAuthorizationCode()
{
    // Start a new process to open the URI
    ProcessStartInfo startInfo = new ProcessStartInfo("explorer", "https://example.com/authenticate");
    startInfo.CreateNoWindow = true; // Don't show the window
    startInfo.UseShellExecute = false;
    Process.Start(startInfo);

    // Read the authorization code from the console
    Console.Write("Enter the authorization code: ");
    string code = Console.ReadLine();

    return code;
}
```
Here's how the method works:

1. We create a `ProcessStartInfo` object to start a new process that opens the specified URI (`https://example.com/authenticate` in this example). We set `CreateNoWindow` to `true` so that the window doesn't appear.
2. We start the process using the `Process.Start()` method.
3. After the process has started, we prompt the user to enter the authorization code by writing a message to the console and reading a line of input using `Console.ReadLine()`.
4. We return the entered code as a string.

Note that this method assumes that the URI is local or can be opened in the default browser (e.g., Explorer). If the URI requires specific handling, you may need to modify the method accordingly.

