2. We parse the input WBXML string using the `wbxml.parse()` method.
3. We iterate through the XML elements in the parsed document using the `forEach()` method.
4. When we find an element with the tag name `<codepage>`, we store its text content as `codePageData`.
5. Finally, we use the `wbxml.decode()` method to decode the code page data using the Windows-1252 encoding scheme.

Note that this implementation assumes that the input WBXML string contains a single `<codepage>` element with the desired code page data. If your WBXML documents have a different structure, you may need to modify the code accordingly.

Also, keep in mind that this is just one possible way to decode an ActiveSync codepage in JavaScript. Depending on your specific use case, you may need to handle errors or edge cases differently.

